1
00:00:02,723 --> 00:00:02,923
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

2
00:00:04,024 --> 00:00:17,293
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the the first one is Anguttara 10.59 and that is a Pabbajja Sutta and it goes by the Buddha addressing the monks, telling them basically how they should train.

3
00:00:18,734 --> 00:00:21,596
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, therefore, monks, this is how you should train.

4
00:00:22,757 --> 00:00:28,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Our mind will be made familiar with going forth as it is, literally, the is-ness of going forth,

5
00:00:34,565 --> 00:00:41,167
[SPEAKER_00]: Our mind will be made familiar with the perception of impermanence.

6
00:00:42,227 --> 00:00:44,828
[SPEAKER_00]: Our mind will be made familiar with the perception of not-self.

7
00:00:46,108 --> 00:00:48,729
[SPEAKER_00]: Our mind will be made familiar with the perception of the unattractive.

8
00:00:49,870 --> 00:00:53,251
[SPEAKER_00]: And our mind will be made familiar with the perception of drawback, danger.

9
00:00:54,608 --> 00:01:00,931
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, furthermore, having known the evenness and unevenness of the world, our mind will be made familiar with that perception.

10
00:01:02,012 --> 00:01:15,178
[SPEAKER_00]: Having known the world's coming-to-be and its uncoming-to-be, I stuck with kind of a more... well, as literal as possible translation, which is bhava vibhava, being and non-being.

11
00:01:15,498 --> 00:01:17,619
[SPEAKER_00]: Our mind will be made familiar with that perception.

12
00:01:18,319 --> 00:01:23,442
[SPEAKER_00]: Having known the world's arising and its fading away, fading out, our mind will be made familiar with that perception.

13
00:01:24,817 --> 00:01:29,762
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, it goes to say, our mind will be made familiar with the perception of abandoning or relinquishment.

14
00:01:31,043 --> 00:01:37,269
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, our mind will be made familiar with the perception of fading out, dispassion, virāga.

15
00:01:38,671 --> 00:01:43,796
[SPEAKER_00]: And the final one is, our mind will be made familiar with the perception of cessation.

16
00:01:44,977 --> 00:01:46,619
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is how the monks should train.

17
00:01:48,158 --> 00:01:58,262
[SPEAKER_00]: Then the Buddha repeats that the monk who trains in these ten perceptions, he can expect one of the two fruits, knowledge and realization in this very life, i.e.

18
00:01:58,362 --> 00:01:59,162
[SPEAKER_00]: full enlightenment.

19
00:02:00,803 --> 00:02:09,847
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, if there is residue left of some unspecified, subtle, too subtle appropriations, he will realize the fruits of non-returning.

20
00:02:11,027 --> 00:02:13,108
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh...

21
00:02:15,010 --> 00:02:37,023
[SPEAKER_00]: If you go back to the beginning, the very first one, it's kind of obvious, often repeated in other suttas, but it's something that, I think, because it's obvious, people will tend to fairly often overlook, which is reminding yourself of the values that made you want to practice in the first place.

22
00:02:39,598 --> 00:02:44,901
[SPEAKER_00]: Remind yourself with your own going forth, as it is.

23
00:02:46,703 --> 00:03:06,917
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you do, if you don't forget your own reasons, like your own aspirations, why you want to practice, why you're committed to the practice, why you're keeping the precepts, why you're doing everything you're doing for the sake of practice, then any unskillful, harmful mental states that arise,

24
00:03:07,717 --> 00:03:15,260
[SPEAKER_00]: They won't be able to, well, completely throw you off the balance, completely overwhelm the right context.

25
00:03:15,901 --> 00:03:29,527
[SPEAKER_00]: It's when people take for granted and kind of forget, drift away from their own reasons because of which they were practicing, that's how these unwholesome states come and

26
00:03:31,478 --> 00:03:51,023
[SPEAKER_00]: A person tolerates them, acts out of them and so on, but if that person were to remind themselves of their own values that brought them there, where they are, in terms of practice, they would... the contradiction between their acts of harmful acts coming of harmful mental states and their values would be too big to ignore.

27
00:03:52,105 --> 00:04:05,819
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's through ignoring your values, that contradiction is being tolerated, and that's when people act unskillfully, unwholesomely, even while deep down, or in the broadest sense, they still very much value their own.

28
00:04:06,733 --> 00:04:22,618
[SPEAKER_00]: Practice and freedom from suffering and everything that the Buddha said, but then every now and then, when unskilled states pressure you, you sort of give in, you forget, because you haven't been frequently bringing up the perceptions of your own values because of which you went forth or you committed to the practice one way or the other.

29
00:04:23,678 --> 00:04:25,439
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the kind of the very first step.

30
00:04:25,559 --> 00:04:31,361
[SPEAKER_00]: It's really just to remind yourself of that and not kind of, oh, I remind myself and then I don't have to do it anymore because I know.

31
00:04:32,318 --> 00:04:32,418
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

32
00:04:32,438 --> 00:04:36,819
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is to go through that recollection of those things.

33
00:04:37,919 --> 00:04:39,659
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to make a point that, yes, yes, I know.

34
00:04:40,419 --> 00:04:41,780
[SPEAKER_00]: But I'm not doing it for myself.

35
00:04:42,100 --> 00:04:43,100
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm doing it for the mind.

36
00:04:43,320 --> 00:04:44,060
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm doing it for citta.

37
00:04:44,080 --> 00:04:45,160
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm doing it for the wild animal.

38
00:04:46,660 --> 00:04:50,421
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm always reminding it of the same boundaries, of the same context.

39
00:04:50,781 --> 00:05:01,443
[SPEAKER_00]: And if sometimes those remindings, those contemplations become, say, too mechanical, you know, you're just slotting into the patterns of

40
00:05:02,686 --> 00:05:06,949
[SPEAKER_00]: mantra-like repeating, because you've been doing it every day, you have to stop that.

41
00:05:07,570 --> 00:05:09,811
[SPEAKER_00]: And you have to, like, refresh it, so to speak.

42
00:05:09,891 --> 00:05:11,012
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to make it alive.

43
00:05:11,032 --> 00:05:14,535
[SPEAKER_00]: These contemplations are not just a

44
00:05:18,145 --> 00:05:26,248
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not an impersonal, theoretical, mental exercise that you would just be going through the motions of thinking or reciting or visualizing.

45
00:05:26,388 --> 00:05:26,528
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

46
00:05:26,749 --> 00:05:27,349
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to feel.

47
00:05:27,529 --> 00:05:35,612
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to feel your own reasons, your own responsibility for your own choice for practice, for following the Buddha's teaching or not.

48
00:05:37,033 --> 00:05:46,917
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when that's always kept alive, so to speak, and made kind of persistent as a context, as a broader context,

49
00:05:47,854 --> 00:05:56,378
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, as the very passage, the same passage says, any arisen harmful, unskilled state cannot cease and take over the mind.

50
00:05:56,478 --> 00:06:12,144
[SPEAKER_00]: So they can arise, they can pressure you, they can get closer and so on, but if the basic foundational values are something you frequently recollect upon, the mind, the animal, cannot be overwhelmed by those pressures, by those wild tendencies.

51
00:06:12,184 --> 00:06:14,105
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's the first step, really.

52
00:06:15,911 --> 00:06:19,754
[SPEAKER_00]: Then goes the same, like, you know, the further refinement, it's okay.

53
00:06:19,794 --> 00:06:20,875
[SPEAKER_00]: Perception of impermanence.

54
00:06:22,076 --> 00:06:38,109
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's like, not just what you think impermanence is, but expanding, including everything, stuff, things that are dear to you, into perception of impermanence, so that impermanence is felt.

55
00:06:38,129 --> 00:06:39,410
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, not just theorized.

56
00:06:40,885 --> 00:06:42,926
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's when it's a perception of impermanence.

57
00:06:42,946 --> 00:06:49,871
[SPEAKER_00]: It refers to those levels, those personal levels, that directly speak to the citta, directly speak to the animal.

58
00:06:50,831 --> 00:06:59,136
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're just pondering about impermanence, like, you know, that modern science has proven, quote-unquote, that's not really the impermanence.

59
00:06:59,156 --> 00:07:05,380
[SPEAKER_00]: The impermanence is that which you feel, which you feel the weight of, if it were to change.

60
00:07:05,540 --> 00:07:08,382
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to find things that evoke that.

61
00:07:09,512 --> 00:07:13,958
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, feed or present those perceptions to that wild animal.

62
00:07:14,199 --> 00:07:15,240
[SPEAKER_00]: At the right time, obviously.

63
00:07:15,280 --> 00:07:18,585
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why we'll address it from the following suttas.

64
00:07:21,072 --> 00:07:39,522
[SPEAKER_00]: Same goes with the not-self, as in, like, non-ownership, it's not mine, cannot belong to me, again, things that are... and you don't do it necessarily only when things, like, bother you and then you just, again, like a mantra, just say, oh, it's not mine, it's not mine, so to just manage whatever is bothering you currently.

65
00:07:39,542 --> 00:07:41,683
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you want to do it where nothing bothers you.

66
00:07:41,703 --> 00:07:42,764
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to dig up things.

67
00:07:42,784 --> 00:07:44,405
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to dig up your complacencies.

68
00:07:45,856 --> 00:07:51,478
[SPEAKER_00]: You're gratuitous, layers of your identity and so on.

69
00:07:51,618 --> 00:07:55,539
[SPEAKER_00]: By the way, this whole thing obviously applies to somebody who is already a sotāpanna.

70
00:07:56,439 --> 00:07:59,160
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're not, you gotta work on seeing the mind.

71
00:07:59,420 --> 00:08:02,021
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't mean that these perceptions cannot be relatable.

72
00:08:02,761 --> 00:08:10,244
[SPEAKER_00]: But you need to understand that without having developed the right view, you are not seeing the animal correctly.

73
00:08:10,564 --> 00:08:13,045
[SPEAKER_00]: You have no sense as to what the citta is.

74
00:08:13,205 --> 00:08:14,065
[SPEAKER_00]: You have ideas.

75
00:08:14,893 --> 00:08:22,518
[SPEAKER_00]: That might correspond to some degree or not, but you do not have the direct insight into the phenomenon of citta and its characteristics.

76
00:08:24,059 --> 00:08:28,222
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that is an absolute prerequisite for all of these three suttas we're going to do today.

77
00:08:29,863 --> 00:08:35,087
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for somebody who's not Sotāpanna, of course, you still go through the same stuff.

78
00:08:35,147 --> 00:08:40,370
[SPEAKER_00]: Just don't confuse it for the practice of a noble disciple.

79
00:08:41,211 --> 00:08:43,993
[SPEAKER_00]: But you could certainly strive through emulate

80
00:08:44,936 --> 00:08:49,879
[SPEAKER_00]: And do these practices of noble disciples as accurately as possible, without deluding yourself into thinking what you're not.

81
00:08:50,440 --> 00:08:53,242
[SPEAKER_00]: And that can actually reveal the citta closer and so on.

82
00:08:53,742 --> 00:08:55,843
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you should still ponder on the perception of impermanence.

83
00:08:55,863 --> 00:09:04,389
[SPEAKER_00]: You still ponder on the perception of not-self, on the perception of unattractive, of the drawbacks and danger in... well, in ownership, in centrality, in everything else.

84
00:09:05,189 --> 00:09:06,750
[SPEAKER_00]: That is what would reveal the citta.

85
00:09:06,770 --> 00:09:08,071
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the practice is not different.

86
00:09:08,131 --> 00:09:08,692
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more like...

87
00:09:10,050 --> 00:09:16,994
[SPEAKER_00]: The reason why I keep repeating that it applies only to somebody who is sotāpanna is because only the sotāpanna or above sees their mind correctly.

88
00:09:18,275 --> 00:09:37,446
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you don't know that that is an absolute requirement for a successful correct practice on a correct level, then any idea you have or you develop on account of reading the suttas and listening to this ajan or that ajan, any idea you develop about impermanence, not-self, unattractive, you think it's what impermanence is.

89
00:09:37,646 --> 00:09:38,807
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't be looking further.

90
00:09:39,525 --> 00:09:42,466
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you think, well, it's clear, so now I'll just contemplate what's clear to me.

91
00:09:43,487 --> 00:09:46,809
[SPEAKER_00]: But the measure of clarity begins when the mind is seen.

92
00:09:48,269 --> 00:10:02,356
[SPEAKER_00]: So, until then, any idea you have about values of going forth, impermanence, not-self, unattractive, and so on, needs not to be taken for granted, mustn't be taken for granted.

93
00:10:02,457 --> 00:10:05,218
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's enough to prevent you from being complacent.

94
00:10:06,255 --> 00:10:12,701
[SPEAKER_00]: But, it's very easy for people, because they become very familiar, to then overlook the fact that they don't even see the citta.

95
00:10:12,721 --> 00:10:13,942
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not sure what it is.

96
00:10:14,763 --> 00:10:17,045
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, they have some ideas, but it's on the level of theory.

97
00:10:17,445 --> 00:10:23,751
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no direct recognition and direct communication through the right means between you and your own wild animal.

98
00:10:24,412 --> 00:10:27,415
[SPEAKER_00]: That is sotāpatti, basically.

99
00:10:28,355 --> 00:10:31,899
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the second one is the evenness and the unevenness of the world.

100
00:10:32,971 --> 00:10:34,552
[SPEAKER_00]: So, people sometimes get confused with this.

101
00:10:35,132 --> 00:10:39,975
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember even some translators, I think, say it's like justice or injustice in the world, which is absolutely ridiculous.

102
00:10:40,015 --> 00:10:46,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, none of these perceptions have anything to do with objects of the world or the world itself.

103
00:10:47,178 --> 00:10:53,122
[SPEAKER_00]: They all have to do with how they influence the mind.

104
00:10:53,262 --> 00:10:55,383
[SPEAKER_00]: When mind is presented with those perceptions,

105
00:10:57,350 --> 00:11:06,700
[SPEAKER_00]: So, evenness and unevenness of the world literally just means, like, balance, imbalance, smoothness or roughness, whichever way you want to translate.

106
00:11:07,160 --> 00:11:09,143
[SPEAKER_00]: Referring to the phenomenon of

107
00:11:10,028 --> 00:11:16,092
[SPEAKER_00]: The entire sense spheres, the entire domain, which is the world, your own body, as the sense organs.

108
00:11:16,773 --> 00:11:19,935
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the broadest sense, not a specific this or that thing.

109
00:11:20,596 --> 00:11:37,308
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sometimes that the broadest sense of your experience, the experience of the world in the widest possible context-wise, can be balanced, can be suitable, can be conducive, can be calming, can be, like, inspiring even.

110
00:11:37,948 --> 00:11:38,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes you won't be.

111
00:11:40,373 --> 00:11:41,213
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's not up to you.

112
00:11:42,614 --> 00:11:48,676
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the evenness and the unevenness of the world, it's pretty much on the level of bhava and vibhava of the world.

113
00:11:48,856 --> 00:11:52,217
[SPEAKER_00]: It's pretty much on the level of coming and going, arising or fading away.

114
00:11:53,238 --> 00:11:58,339
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the reason why it's spoken of in these general terms is that's what these phenomena are.

115
00:11:58,800 --> 00:12:08,283
[SPEAKER_00]: If you still think that the specific things in the world are the world, you are not seeing the influence of that broad context of the world, that influence that it has on the mind.

116
00:12:09,243 --> 00:12:21,052
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why sense restraint is necessary, virtue is necessary to move that threshold and withdraw from that dependence on specificity of your central object, central perception, central observations.

117
00:12:21,913 --> 00:12:23,894
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the broader phenomena become apparent.

118
00:12:24,775 --> 00:12:31,560
[SPEAKER_00]: And the broadest phenomena would be pretty much anything, the entire domain of external, which is what stands for the world.

119
00:12:33,382 --> 00:12:47,351
[SPEAKER_00]: And sometimes, as I said, that might be smooth and agreeable and balanced, but you need to recognize, aha, in the broadest sense, circumstances are fine, my mind mustn't get complacent.

120
00:12:48,952 --> 00:12:55,377
[SPEAKER_00]: In regard to that level, in regard to that subtle, broad, almost invisible context for an untrained mind.

121
00:12:56,283 --> 00:13:14,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Equally, when that equally broad world becomes uneven, becomes disturbing, becomes rough, whatever, whichever way it would be manifested specifically through the elements of this or that or any sort of

122
00:13:15,635 --> 00:13:22,516
[SPEAKER_00]: Bother, pressure, disturbance, that comes from the width of that world, of that phenomenon of the world, same.

123
00:13:22,896 --> 00:13:23,136
[SPEAKER_00]: Aha!

124
00:13:23,597 --> 00:13:25,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, I mustn't let my mind be affected by that.

125
00:13:26,697 --> 00:13:41,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, obviously, somebody trains in regard to those broad, factual, real as such phenomena, perceptions of phenomena of the world, anything inferior, as the suttas, those suttas about casinos, for example, as they say, anything inferior cannot disturb you.

126
00:13:43,014 --> 00:13:58,909
[SPEAKER_00]: So, any specific thing that comes on account of an even experience of the entire world, that you are obviously detached from sufficiently enough, anything specific that comes, that cannot bother you, because the framework remains complete detachment.

127
00:13:58,969 --> 00:13:59,169
[SPEAKER_00]: Same.

128
00:13:59,529 --> 00:14:05,915
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're already not bothered on account of the roughness and unevenness of the world, anything specific

129
00:14:06,740 --> 00:14:14,648
[SPEAKER_00]: that attacks you here or there cannot disturb the mind any more than the broad world would have tried to disturb it and the mind is immovable in regard to it.

130
00:14:15,309 --> 00:14:16,510
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's kind of an upgrade.

131
00:14:17,533 --> 00:14:23,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Of these perceptions of, you know, that start on that immediate level, impermanence, not-self, unattractive, danger...

132
00:14:23,294 --> 00:14:24,834
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, this is also on the immediate level.

133
00:14:25,374 --> 00:14:36,676
[SPEAKER_00]: But, in order to be discerning the actual phenomenon of the world without falling into abstraction or theory or trapped in a specified sense, that needs development, that needs refinement.

134
00:14:36,776 --> 00:14:45,258
[SPEAKER_00]: So, evenness, unevenness, coming-to-be, uncoming-to-be, pretty much, if you train your mind in regard to the subtle higher perceptions,

135
00:14:47,020 --> 00:14:49,061
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, arahantship or non-return.

136
00:14:49,821 --> 00:14:53,362
[SPEAKER_00]: There cannot be any lower fetters remaining, really.

137
00:14:53,402 --> 00:14:58,324
[SPEAKER_00]: They would have been addressed with first, what, four perceptions.

138
00:14:58,624 --> 00:15:00,884
[SPEAKER_00]: And the same goes perception of abandoning, fading out.

139
00:15:01,024 --> 00:15:05,326
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just this, again, for most people, in the initial stage, those are abstractions.

140
00:15:06,186 --> 00:15:08,146
[SPEAKER_00]: Cessation, letting go, just let go.

141
00:15:08,186 --> 00:15:09,767
[SPEAKER_00]: Almost like a poetic inspiration.

142
00:15:10,660 --> 00:15:27,490
[SPEAKER_00]: But for somebody with the right view, who is seeing the mind correctly, who has been training that wild animal correctly on the right level, in that peripheral sphere, how we often talk about, thoughts of letting go are actually communicating letting go to the animal.

143
00:15:29,231 --> 00:15:30,692
[SPEAKER_00]: For non-sotāpāna, they don't.

144
00:15:31,192 --> 00:15:34,934
[SPEAKER_00]: They just communicate wishful thinking, because they don't even see, they don't even have the animal there.

145
00:15:35,435 --> 00:15:35,635
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

146
00:15:36,944 --> 00:15:43,290
[SPEAKER_00]: Thoughts of fading out, thoughts of dispassion, relinquishment, abandoning, are actually speaking to the animal.

147
00:15:44,891 --> 00:15:56,561
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is no more abstraction, there is no more separation, because the mind is made progressively more and more pliable, more and more obedient, more and more receptive to your own commands, to your own instructions.

148
00:15:57,642 --> 00:16:02,126
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that pretty much kind of sums up this sutta, which would sum up the entire training.

149
00:16:02,206 --> 00:16:02,466
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's

150
00:16:03,462 --> 00:16:18,490
[SPEAKER_00]: Through feeding the right perceptions to the mind, that has been rightly contained, through being established in virtue, established in sense restraint, which is what Sotāpattī is being correctly established, then the mind will have no āsavas remaining, if the whole thing is done successfully.

151
00:16:19,531 --> 00:16:22,793
[SPEAKER_00]: And even if you fail to some degree, you'll be at least a non-returner.

152
00:16:24,134 --> 00:16:27,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is a pretty good deal.

153
00:16:27,195 --> 00:16:31,137
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the second sutta is Sītibhāva Sutta, which is a

154
00:16:33,208 --> 00:16:39,071
[SPEAKER_00]: A monk endowed with six qualities is incapable of realizing the unsupplied state of cooling, nibbāna.

155
00:16:40,252 --> 00:16:42,173
[SPEAKER_00]: So, which are the six?

156
00:16:43,633 --> 00:16:52,398
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, eh... A monk, at a time when the mind should be restrained or held back, he does not restrain it.

157
00:16:53,518 --> 00:16:56,960
[SPEAKER_00]: At a time the mind should be released or let go or extended,

158
00:16:58,450 --> 00:16:59,130
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't do that.

159
00:16:59,190 --> 00:16:59,991
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't release it.

160
00:17:00,031 --> 00:17:00,771
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't let go of it.

161
00:17:01,311 --> 00:17:02,052
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't let it go.

162
00:17:02,852 --> 00:17:06,694
[SPEAKER_00]: At a time when the mind should be gladdened or uplifted, he doesn't gladden or uplift it.

163
00:17:06,914 --> 00:17:11,917
[SPEAKER_00]: At a time when the mind should be closely watched with equanimity, he doesn't do that.

164
00:17:15,638 --> 00:17:24,703
[SPEAKER_00]: That person who doesn't do those things is the one who is inclined to the inferior, to the lower, and is the one who delights in identity and embodiment.

165
00:17:25,990 --> 00:17:28,511
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, this refers to somebody who sees their mind.

166
00:17:31,553 --> 00:17:44,380
[SPEAKER_00]: That is just so much taken for granted, but the suttas very clearly, every time, outline the whole necessity of citta, nimitta,

167
00:17:45,963 --> 00:18:02,295
[SPEAKER_00]: For Sotāpati, Sotāpati being identical to the establishment in virtue, sense restraint, on account of that, you discern the signs of mind, you withdraw from the dependence on the sense objects and so on, through knowing your intentions, everything we often talk about all the time.

168
00:18:03,315 --> 00:18:10,220
[SPEAKER_00]: But see, even if somebody does that, yeah, they got the right view, they see the mind, but the mind still needs taming.

169
00:18:11,021 --> 00:18:12,142
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, you caught the animal.

170
00:18:12,910 --> 00:18:13,730
[SPEAKER_00]: You fenced it off.

171
00:18:14,150 --> 00:18:17,311
[SPEAKER_00]: You got the wild dog in, but it's still wild.

172
00:18:18,231 --> 00:18:27,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Automatically, it won't be as wild as when it was allowed to roam freely before, but the tendencies of its wilderness and stuff are still there.

173
00:18:27,514 --> 00:18:28,694
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the training now.

174
00:18:29,575 --> 00:18:30,255
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what Buddha says.

175
00:18:30,275 --> 00:18:34,496
[SPEAKER_00]: The one who doesn't invest effort in training this animal,

176
00:18:35,982 --> 00:18:43,767
[SPEAKER_00]: Doing the right training at the right time, releasing, restraining, gladdening, closely watching with neutrality.

177
00:18:44,027 --> 00:18:50,090
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he's the one who still inclines towards the inferior, towards the sense objects, sense essentiality, sense fears.

178
00:18:51,171 --> 00:18:53,152
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why he's not doing the training.

179
00:18:55,358 --> 00:18:58,219
[SPEAKER_00]: And, he's the one who still delights in identity.

180
00:18:58,319 --> 00:19:02,501
[SPEAKER_00]: And, whenever sakāya is used, it has that sense of embodiment.

181
00:19:03,041 --> 00:19:08,143
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not attavāda, which is what differentiates a putujana from a sotāpanna.

182
00:19:09,043 --> 00:19:16,466
[SPEAKER_00]: That implicit, deeply, kind of tacit, ingrained view that sense of self exists independent of your experience.

183
00:19:16,526 --> 00:19:17,266
[SPEAKER_00]: Your sense of self.

184
00:19:18,187 --> 00:19:21,088
[SPEAKER_00]: Which, by default, then projects it independent of the five aggregates.

185
00:19:21,932 --> 00:19:23,233
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the Putujana's view.

186
00:19:23,614 --> 00:19:37,269
[SPEAKER_00]: Sotāpanna would have abandoned that view, but he still very much has that sense of strong... strong sense, or depending on, you know, how undeveloped he still is, can have a strong sense of I am that is inseparable from the body, from the senses.

187
00:19:37,329 --> 00:19:39,391
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's always embodied in this form.

188
00:19:40,157 --> 00:19:48,843
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha often encouraged monks to, you know, be disgusted with their own body and contemplate and say, like, a young man or a woman have a snake, dead snake, wrapped around their neck.

189
00:19:48,863 --> 00:19:50,964
[SPEAKER_00]: Wouldn't they be disgusted or repelled with that form?

190
00:19:51,725 --> 00:20:00,591
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how you should practice, to just undermine any lingering sense of that embodied identity, to put it that way.

191
00:20:01,611 --> 00:20:09,657
[SPEAKER_00]: So, or, as this sutta says, if you invest the time and training to restrain the wild animal that you caught, when it's time to restrain it,

192
00:20:11,322 --> 00:20:34,243
[SPEAKER_00]: To let it go, when it's time to let it go, allow it to roam about a bit, to uplift it, when it's time to uplift it, and to watch it with a neutral eye, when it's time to do that, then you would be abandoning the inferior, and you would be uprooting, and you wouldn't be delighting in embodiment of your own, you know, with your own senses and so on.

193
00:20:35,352 --> 00:20:40,854
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the monk, who hasn't basically abandoned these six qualities, is still incapable of realizing nibbāna.

194
00:20:41,114 --> 00:20:46,216
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he might stay sotāpanna the whole life, but that's because he's not actually investing effort to train the animal.

195
00:20:46,336 --> 00:20:51,978
[SPEAKER_00]: And that was the case of many householders, who then, due to one reason or the other, returned back to the lay life.

196
00:20:52,638 --> 00:21:13,867
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, after the Buddha would have instructed them rightly, saw the citta, trained them, they got the right view, but then they were happy with that and, you know, they were happy with the fact that their animal was seen, somewhat contained, free from peril in the future and so on, but they didn't invest the further effort.

197
00:21:14,007 --> 00:21:17,328
[SPEAKER_00]: They could have been invested and you could have finished the whole thing once and for all.

198
00:21:19,922 --> 00:21:27,506
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, obviously, the rest of the sutta, the Buddha goes and repeats the opposite qualities, where the monk does restrain, where he should restrain, and so on.

199
00:21:28,027 --> 00:21:30,328
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he realizes nibbāna as a result of it.

200
00:21:30,468 --> 00:21:40,433
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing left for him to tame, because the citta, the animal, has been fully instructed, subdued, tamed, and then liberated.

201
00:21:42,795 --> 00:21:47,777
[SPEAKER_00]: And the last sutta is the nimitta sutta, Anguttara 302.

202
00:21:54,004 --> 00:22:02,692
[SPEAKER_00]: The other suttas don't specify the higher mind training, but you could see that it pretty much goes in that direction.

203
00:22:03,012 --> 00:22:11,480
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, the perception of evenness, unevenness, bhāva, vibhāva, already starts pertaining to these higher factors.

204
00:22:12,301 --> 00:22:14,683
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's already a form of ādi-citta training.

205
00:22:16,043 --> 00:22:33,528
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this sutta specifically states that the monk who wants to develop that Adi-Citta training, the higher mind, so let alone reminding yourself why you became a monk and keeping those values and being established in precepts and virtue and sense restraint, seeing the basic citta nimitta and stuff, this is now, like, say you've been doing that for a while,

206
00:22:34,560 --> 00:22:43,045
[SPEAKER_00]: And now you're entering a sphere of this subtle communication, where, as I said, it's enough for you to think rightly about relinquishment, and the animal is getting that message.

207
00:22:43,565 --> 00:22:44,305
[SPEAKER_00]: It's relinquishing.

208
00:22:45,106 --> 00:22:45,906
[SPEAKER_00]: It's understanding you.

209
00:22:46,086 --> 00:22:47,007
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to do more.

210
00:22:48,307 --> 00:22:48,588
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

211
00:22:49,571 --> 00:22:59,415
[SPEAKER_00]: This sutta is for the monk who is devoted to such higher mind training and there are three signs that he should often be brought... that he should bring to his mind from time to time.

212
00:23:00,295 --> 00:23:04,017
[SPEAKER_00]: And you'll see that all these three signs are quite general phenomena.

213
00:23:04,217 --> 00:23:05,477
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not specific things.

214
00:23:06,077 --> 00:23:12,900
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, as I said, when it comes to the Adhicitta level of communication, specificity would have been abandoned a long time ago.

215
00:23:13,929 --> 00:23:19,235
[SPEAKER_00]: You are now talking about general phenomena, accurate perceptions of general phenomena, real as such.

216
00:23:20,196 --> 00:23:27,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Which, for most people, that still depend on lower thresholds of specificity, would be just abstract.

217
00:23:27,764 --> 00:23:29,085
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just abstract theorizing.

218
00:23:29,105 --> 00:23:30,526
[SPEAKER_00]: It does not stand for those phenomena.

219
00:23:31,547 --> 00:23:32,969
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the three signs

220
00:23:34,770 --> 00:23:49,020
[SPEAKER_00]: The three adhicitta nimitta, that you can talk to your animal, are from time to time the steadiness sign should be brought to mind, the energizing sign should be brought to mind, and the equanimity sign should be brought to mind.

221
00:23:50,301 --> 00:23:58,267
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if a monk, who is devoted to the higher training, exclusively brings his mind only the steadiness sign, then the mind would become sluggish.

222
00:23:58,967 --> 00:24:03,491
[SPEAKER_00]: If he exclusively brings only the energizing sign, the mind would become agitated.

223
00:24:04,591 --> 00:24:20,452
[SPEAKER_00]: And if he exclusively brings only the equanimity sign, then the mind will not become rightly composed, rightly balanced for the understanding, the exhaustion of the outflows, which basically means full correct understanding of the asavas, which is what really ends them.

224
00:24:23,198 --> 00:24:40,662
[SPEAKER_00]: But when the mind... when the monk is devoted to such higher mind training and he brings from time to time the steadiness sign, the energizing sign, the equanimity sign, then his citta, his wild animal, well, ceases to be wild, becomes pliant, workable, bright and not brittle.

225
00:24:42,283 --> 00:24:45,503
[SPEAKER_00]: It becomes rightly composed for the exhaustion of the outflows.

226
00:24:45,843 --> 00:24:48,304
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the Buddha gives a nice example of the goldsmith

227
00:24:49,681 --> 00:24:54,864
[SPEAKER_00]: But if he only does only one of these things, the gold would either burn, or be too hard, or wouldn't mature.

228
00:24:54,924 --> 00:24:56,545
[SPEAKER_00]: But when he does the right things at the right time,

229
00:25:16,152 --> 00:25:22,014
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, that goal becomes completely pliable, cleared of impurities, and then he can make anything he wants out of it.

230
00:25:22,555 --> 00:25:34,099
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the whole point of training the mind, because, fundamentally, the mind doesn't want to see the full extent of unknown ability, the full extent of the peril of sensuality, the full extent of the dukkha.

231
00:25:35,440 --> 00:25:36,180
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can't force it.

232
00:25:37,269 --> 00:25:46,883
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, you can get a wild dog, you can teach it tricks, and you can tame it to some extent, but deep down, it's still gonna remain somewhat an animal.

233
00:25:48,138 --> 00:26:00,786
[SPEAKER_00]: And for those things to be seen on the level that results in arahantship, there mustn't be any part of that animal, any part of that citta, that is not turned towards it and accepting it and and seeing through it.

234
00:26:01,106 --> 00:26:05,149
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if there is any holding back, means the mind... the gold is not entirely pliable.

235
00:26:05,209 --> 00:26:06,590
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind has not been fully trained.

236
00:26:06,970 --> 00:26:09,472
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the point of this whole whole sutta.

237
00:26:09,512 --> 00:26:16,877
[SPEAKER_00]: To explain that for those residues of of of cravings and asavas, the right things must be done at the right time.

238
00:26:18,330 --> 00:26:21,111
[SPEAKER_00]: And that will then eventually bring that mind to full pliability.

239
00:26:21,131 --> 00:26:44,941
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when you direct it towards the dukkha, towards the full understanding of freedom and so on, well, towards basically having it sink in what you would have understood as a sotāpanna already, the way out of suffering, the full weight of that to sink in, the animal must actually have no reservations towards it, not holding back, like those other suttas when they would have compared an elephant that's being ready for the battle.

240
00:26:45,441 --> 00:26:46,922
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when he's seemingly trained,

241
00:26:47,462 --> 00:26:58,960
[SPEAKER_00]: They would make him hold something in its trunk and then they would poke it with spears and stuff to see if it's still gonna kind of flinch back and hide some parts of it or allow everything to be exposed because it's fearless.

242
00:26:59,601 --> 00:27:00,563
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's exactly the same.

243
00:27:02,386 --> 00:27:06,410
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and then he says, yeah, you can make any ornament and it's same for the monk.

244
00:27:06,650 --> 00:27:22,226
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if he does the... if he brings the right signs at the right time, then whether he wishes... basically, if there is any opening for any of the superhuman knowledges, if he directs his mind towards that, the mind will get it, the mind will see it, the mind will understand it.

245
00:27:23,347 --> 00:27:32,835
[SPEAKER_00]: Fundamentally, obviously, the most important one was when the monk realizes that his mind is fully pliable, he just directs his mind and says, may I, having realized the exhaustion of the old flaws, enter and dwell in it.

246
00:27:33,656 --> 00:27:43,483
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not just, you know, occasionally get a taste from it, like a noble disciple would, but enter, dwell in it, without mind ever returning from that state of coolness.

247
00:27:44,084 --> 00:27:45,165
[SPEAKER_00]: So, full dispassion.

248
00:27:45,585 --> 00:27:47,847
[SPEAKER_00]: For that, as I said, the mind needs to be fully pliable.

249
00:27:49,440 --> 00:27:54,344
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that kind of concludes the three suttas.

250
00:27:55,845 --> 00:28:04,391
[SPEAKER_00]: And let me now just go through some questions that I might not have addressed during this reading.

251
00:28:04,411 --> 00:28:09,334
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's just see.

252
00:28:13,037 --> 00:28:14,718
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll answer that, answer that.

253
00:28:21,195 --> 00:28:21,695
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't see that.

254
00:28:29,257 --> 00:28:46,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so a question I got from a few people was kind of... What is the measure of this right time, right tune regarding this last sutta?

255
00:28:49,058 --> 00:28:53,020
[SPEAKER_00]: And then also, another question was really about... people are confused.

256
00:28:53,180 --> 00:28:55,081
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you be overdoing, Upeka?

257
00:28:55,101 --> 00:29:00,085
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you be overdoing the equanimity?

258
00:29:00,185 --> 00:29:06,569
[SPEAKER_00]: Because... well, it just seems like if you look upon everything with equanimity, it would be fine.

259
00:29:07,670 --> 00:29:12,953
[SPEAKER_00]: But really, when it comes to the... well, the Adhichitta training, as the Sutta says,

260
00:29:16,070 --> 00:29:30,782
[SPEAKER_00]: When it says the perceptions of... sorry, the nimittas of steadying the animal, or the nimittas of letting it go, it's what I've been giving... talking about in those examples of... again, taming the animal, whether it's the elephant or the dog, whatever you're familiar with.

261
00:29:32,303 --> 00:29:33,203
[SPEAKER_00]: The goal is to train it.

262
00:29:33,984 --> 00:29:35,185
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you got a wild animal.

263
00:29:35,365 --> 00:29:36,366
[SPEAKER_00]: The goal is to train it.

264
00:29:36,646 --> 00:29:38,568
[SPEAKER_00]: The goal is to make it listen to you.

265
00:29:39,391 --> 00:29:47,234
[SPEAKER_00]: So that you don't have to keep the fences up, keep the alarms going on, keep the GPS trackers on and stuff, because it always escapes and stuff like that.

266
00:29:48,294 --> 00:29:53,296
[SPEAKER_00]: So, initially, of course, you need to trap the animal, like what the Sutta says.

267
00:29:53,616 --> 00:30:01,358
[SPEAKER_00]: It traps the elephant and then elephant shits and pisses and screams and wants to kill everything that comes near it, because it's tied to a strong post.

268
00:30:02,259 --> 00:30:03,319
[SPEAKER_00]: But then it calms down.

269
00:30:04,542 --> 00:30:06,523
[SPEAKER_00]: And when it comes down, that's when you start to approach it.

270
00:30:07,064 --> 00:30:15,970
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for somebody who hasn't been restrained, any form of restraint, especially when they hear about eight precepts, their mind will be urinating and defecating and screaming at them to not do it.

271
00:30:16,590 --> 00:30:20,973
[SPEAKER_00]: And they'll try to find any reason so that they don't have to do it and still pretend that they're practicing.

272
00:30:21,053 --> 00:30:24,776
[SPEAKER_00]: But deep down, they know practice begins with trapping the animal.

273
00:30:25,416 --> 00:30:29,899
[SPEAKER_00]: And for that, most of the animals won't be happy about, at least initially.

274
00:30:31,340 --> 00:30:32,301
[SPEAKER_00]: But once that is done,

275
00:30:34,684 --> 00:30:45,472
[SPEAKER_00]: You would realize... because initially, when you hear that from a non... from the position of non-restraint, you think that the entire practice, all the way to nibbāna, is just about saying no to everything.

276
00:30:46,372 --> 00:30:52,637
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just about tying the animal down, and choking it down, and keeping it down, and whenever he wants to move, you quickly restrain.

277
00:30:53,137 --> 00:30:55,779
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's like, well, no, that would just asphyxiate the animal.

278
00:30:56,140 --> 00:30:57,240
[SPEAKER_00]: You're still not going to train it.

279
00:30:57,541 --> 00:31:00,903
[SPEAKER_00]: That is pretty much the self-mortification.

280
00:31:01,423 --> 00:31:01,624
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

281
00:31:02,554 --> 00:31:03,757
[SPEAKER_00]: Initially, it might feel like that.

282
00:31:05,039 --> 00:31:13,696
[SPEAKER_00]: You put that aside, put that fear aside, start doing it, and then you realize, okay, so the animal is not as wild, does not want to go as far.

283
00:31:14,954 --> 00:31:20,078
[SPEAKER_00]: I need to let it move a bit within this confined, safe, say, fenced-off area.

284
00:31:20,579 --> 00:31:25,263
[SPEAKER_00]: I need to let it move because I actually need to learn how to communicate to it and it needs to learn how to listen to me.

285
00:31:25,703 --> 00:31:37,493
[SPEAKER_00]: If all I'm doing is just keeping it down, then not even allowing it to walk on the leash, like, the only message it's getting from me is no, no, no, which means just it's not going to give up its resistance.

286
00:31:38,202 --> 00:31:44,488
[SPEAKER_00]: You might be subdued and, you know, lose the will to live, but it won't learn for itself.

287
00:31:44,829 --> 00:31:46,490
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the point.

288
00:31:46,551 --> 00:31:53,698
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when this sutta talks about... say somebody is not doing it that blindly anymore, recognizes there is a balance, there is space to move.

289
00:31:54,218 --> 00:31:55,460
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's too tight, you loosen it.

290
00:31:55,500 --> 00:31:57,141
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's too loose, you tighten it, and so on.

291
00:32:00,584 --> 00:32:08,750
[SPEAKER_00]: The higher training, when it says, like, you know, you steady the mind, when it needs steadying, you can think of it like you have your dog on a leash.

292
00:32:09,270 --> 00:32:15,855
[SPEAKER_00]: It's already somewhat trained, but, obviously, it still has triggers, it still has tendencies, same like your mind does.

293
00:32:16,976 --> 00:32:22,560
[SPEAKER_00]: So, steadying the mind rightly would be if, say, that dog wants to go at something.

294
00:32:24,081 --> 00:32:26,703
[SPEAKER_00]: You pull the leash and the dog gets the message.

295
00:32:27,612 --> 00:32:31,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know, before you had to pull the leash and stomp on the leash and hold it down with your hands.

296
00:32:32,453 --> 00:32:35,314
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, it's not that bad, but you still gotta hold the leash.

297
00:32:36,274 --> 00:32:43,096
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, deep down, there are tendencies in that animal that are not uprooted and you would be foolish to completely trust it.

298
00:32:44,056 --> 00:32:47,857
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you hold the leash down when it's necessary.

299
00:32:48,837 --> 00:32:53,238
[SPEAKER_00]: When the animal wants to go, you pull the leash, gets the message, sits down, but you hold the leash.

300
00:32:54,595 --> 00:33:03,942
[SPEAKER_00]: The second example is... How does the sutra say?

301
00:33:03,982 --> 00:33:06,964
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the second example is the animal doesn't want to move.

302
00:33:08,345 --> 00:33:13,629
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it just doesn't want to move, doesn't want to go in the direction you know it should go.

303
00:33:13,749 --> 00:33:17,132
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, these are the perceptions that it needs to become familiar with.

304
00:33:18,053 --> 00:33:23,437
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, in order to train the animal, or your mind, yourself, out of your triggers, you need to

305
00:33:24,332 --> 00:33:28,493
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, rightly, within the right environment, expose yourself to those things.

306
00:33:28,553 --> 00:33:31,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Mentally, recognize the perceptions that you don't want to think about.

307
00:33:32,275 --> 00:33:35,356
[SPEAKER_00]: Death, illness, sickness, aging, and so on.

308
00:33:35,376 --> 00:33:37,836
[SPEAKER_00]: The perception of impermanence, like what the previous sutta said.

309
00:33:38,717 --> 00:33:42,098
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that requires effort, because the animal will not willingly go there.

310
00:33:42,898 --> 00:33:46,359
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe occasionally, but generally, you need to... No, no, come on.

311
00:33:46,499 --> 00:33:47,359
[SPEAKER_00]: It's time to look at this.

312
00:33:47,439 --> 00:33:52,101
[SPEAKER_00]: It's time to, you know, go to the... go to the... to the different aspects of the training.

313
00:33:53,022 --> 00:33:55,103
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you still hold the leash and you start to guide it.

314
00:33:55,984 --> 00:33:56,564
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it goes.

315
00:33:57,065 --> 00:33:57,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Then it's fine.

316
00:33:58,145 --> 00:34:06,151
[SPEAKER_00]: If it starts... See, but if all you do is pull it with the leash wherever you want to go, then it's going to become too agitated and it's going to go too far.

317
00:34:06,751 --> 00:34:14,797
[SPEAKER_00]: And same, if all you're doing is holding the leash back whenever it wants to move, regardless of what it is moving towards, then it's going to become sluggish.

318
00:34:14,837 --> 00:34:19,440
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to lose their will, its will to move towards anything.

319
00:34:20,238 --> 00:34:22,799
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's like too much steadiness or too much energy.

320
00:34:23,960 --> 00:34:28,422
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all measured by the state of the mind, not by what you think steadiness or energy is.

321
00:34:29,362 --> 00:34:40,006
[SPEAKER_00]: And from that point of view, the equanimity... the sign of equanimity would be when the animal is neither too agitated... and literally, you could think...

322
00:34:40,086 --> 00:34:43,488
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know if you ever trained a dog, but that's exactly how you... when you would do it.

323
00:34:44,825 --> 00:34:54,014
[SPEAKER_00]: When you know the dog is neither too agitated nor too kind of lazy or just shut down away from you because you've been sort of restraining it too much.

324
00:34:54,234 --> 00:34:59,820
[SPEAKER_00]: When it's kind of there, listening to you with a degree of freedom, you let go of the leash.

325
00:35:00,801 --> 00:35:01,962
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the equanimity part.

326
00:35:03,391 --> 00:35:05,612
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when it's rightly stated, you let go of the leash.

327
00:35:06,132 --> 00:35:12,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, fundamentally, the point of the entire training is that animal starts to prefer the right things that you've been teaching it.

328
00:35:13,315 --> 00:35:17,917
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when you will be liberated, animal will be liberated, and you will know that it's liberated.

329
00:35:18,638 --> 00:35:25,921
[SPEAKER_00]: When you don't have to be fabricating it, doing it anymore, through, you know, controlling the leash and so on.

330
00:35:26,803 --> 00:35:30,905
[SPEAKER_00]: And from that example, you can also see how it would be sometimes wrong time to do that.

331
00:35:31,526 --> 00:35:41,292
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if the animal is not quite balanced and you let go of the leash, well, it's gonna run away into the directions it shouldn't and then you have to undo that and bring it back and restart.

332
00:35:42,312 --> 00:35:46,135
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how it's not like too much equanimity, but it's more like at the wrong time.

333
00:35:47,735 --> 00:35:48,056
[SPEAKER_00]: And same.

334
00:35:48,516 --> 00:35:55,040
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're restraining the animal, steadying the leash, pulling it back, when it's not going into anything unwholesome,

335
00:35:56,136 --> 00:35:57,077
[SPEAKER_00]: That's at the wrong time.

336
00:35:57,197 --> 00:36:03,580
[SPEAKER_00]: You're applying the certain sign, well, the correct sign, but at the wrong time.

337
00:36:03,720 --> 00:36:13,866
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're energizing it where it's already agitated and you are pulling it with the leash to move more, again, sure, it's a useful thing to do sometimes, but not at the wrong time.

338
00:36:16,608 --> 00:36:23,112
[SPEAKER_00]: So, from that point of view, you wouldn't want to just drop the leash every single time and hope that animal will do the right thing.

339
00:36:23,969 --> 00:36:24,089
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

340
00:36:24,249 --> 00:36:29,173
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to have developed everything beforehand and then you know when there is, like, less risk.

341
00:36:29,814 --> 00:36:30,534
[SPEAKER_00]: Same like with the drone.

342
00:36:30,554 --> 00:36:31,655
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, I could try now.

343
00:36:32,716 --> 00:36:35,798
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if it runs, it won't go too far or this...

344
00:36:35,898 --> 00:36:37,099
[SPEAKER_00]: I could let go of the leash now.

345
00:36:37,560 --> 00:36:40,462
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, just let go of telling it anything and see where it goes.

346
00:36:42,083 --> 00:36:50,850
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you would already be fairly familiar with its signs and features that indicate the directions of its intentions and behavior way before it even acts.

347
00:36:52,123 --> 00:37:02,311
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know, you get a dog, you never had any experience with training the animal, it would be, you know, you need for it to, like, jump in rage to realize, oh, it's angry, let me pull it back.

348
00:37:04,333 --> 00:37:15,881
[SPEAKER_00]: But the more familiar you become with the animal, with the dog, with its signs and features and little ticks and hints, you don't even, like, you would, you just need to look at its eyes, for example, and you know already, like, oh,

349
00:37:17,218 --> 00:37:18,119
[SPEAKER_00]: It's wound up.

350
00:37:18,419 --> 00:37:22,900
[SPEAKER_00]: If I let it now, it will go so far, it will probably attack something or somebody.

351
00:37:23,261 --> 00:37:24,481
[SPEAKER_00]: And you could see the same with the mind.

352
00:37:24,961 --> 00:37:31,724
[SPEAKER_00]: Initially, you needed like full swings between sensuality, intense cravings, ill will, hindrances.

353
00:37:32,224 --> 00:37:38,887
[SPEAKER_00]: But as you start training the animal rightly, you don't even need to go that far to know that it would go that far.

354
00:37:40,140 --> 00:37:48,306
[SPEAKER_00]: So, little position of the tail, posturing, the way it got up, the way it looks, it's like you already know where that would go.

355
00:37:48,726 --> 00:37:51,768
[SPEAKER_00]: You apply the restraint, if that's what's called for.

356
00:37:53,429 --> 00:38:08,900
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you are to learn to, if you have learned how to see those subtle signs, adhicitta level signs, the higher mind signs, then you would know even more accurately when it's the most safe to let go of the leash completely.

357
00:38:11,072 --> 00:38:18,341
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why that practice of equanimity as the third sign obviously requires that development of balance beforehand, like, pretty much thoroughly.

358
00:38:19,342 --> 00:38:29,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, you could still, like, you know, make a mistake and leave it... let go of the leash for too long and then ignore the signs of the animal, but that's how you would yourself learn and become more and more accurate in training that animal.

359
00:38:29,875 --> 00:38:44,881
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's recognizing the little mental inclinations of your own heart, of your own, that, as I said, the broad kind of evocative, emotional, the broadest phenomena, where it leans to way before the feeling would have even fully developed.

360
00:38:46,241 --> 00:38:48,282
[SPEAKER_00]: And recognize, no, you don't go there.

361
00:38:49,282 --> 00:38:52,064
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's nothing unwholesome, you can go there.

362
00:38:52,964 --> 00:38:55,705
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's neutral, okay, we're going there, but together.

363
00:38:57,492 --> 00:38:58,753
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I will let you go there.

364
00:38:58,873 --> 00:39:02,415
[SPEAKER_00]: I will even drop the leash, if I'm... if I'm confident in your balance.

365
00:39:02,995 --> 00:39:04,576
[SPEAKER_00]: But, obviously, I will walk with you.

366
00:39:05,157 --> 00:39:08,098
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not just letting you roam completely.

367
00:39:08,339 --> 00:39:25,309
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that's why I keep saying to people, practically speaking, that's why you always need to... Well, the measure, really, of the right direction of the practice or the wrong direction of the practice is knowing your intentions in regard to everything and anything you want to do and recognizing

368
00:39:26,135 --> 00:39:41,284
[SPEAKER_00]: That there is never a compromise or the external allowance that justifies... that would justify you not putting those intentions as the sole factor for deciding whether your entire projection of your forthcoming actions would be wholesome or unwholesome.

369
00:39:42,225 --> 00:39:52,831
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you could be doing what the whole world agrees you should do, if you are doing it for the... from the intention that's rooted in either greed, aversion, distraction or any hindrance, it's unwholesome.

370
00:39:53,232 --> 00:39:55,233
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter how it measures externally.

371
00:39:56,743 --> 00:40:07,892
[SPEAKER_00]: And same, if the whole world agrees you shouldn't do something because, no, that's wrong, within the precepts, obviously, if your intention is correct, within the precepts, then you know it's fine.

372
00:40:08,492 --> 00:40:10,214
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I'm not doing it for the wrong reasons.

373
00:40:10,854 --> 00:40:14,357
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's especially true when it comes to these views about what practice is and isn't.

374
00:40:15,147 --> 00:40:22,514
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, you shouldn't do that, you know, everybody says, you know, you can do this, but it's like, you could be doing the right things externally, but internally for the wrong reasons.

375
00:40:23,014 --> 00:40:40,611
[SPEAKER_00]: And if no practice tells you that actually, yeah, you're gonna start wrongly one way or the other, but if no practice tells you to start looking at your reasons as the decisive factor, your thoughts, your intentions, you're never gonna get the practice kind of picking up on the right level.

376
00:40:42,452 --> 00:40:45,014
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that's this whole... the whole... the whole point of this training.

377
00:40:45,414 --> 00:40:56,622
[SPEAKER_00]: The whole point of this taming would be that if you start recognizing your own inclinations, your own intentions, that is already what animal is... is seeing or pushing for and that's already the message it's getting through to that animal.

378
00:40:58,043 --> 00:41:05,728
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't even... as I said, you don't even need to have the perceptions of sensuality or the perceptions of ill-will or things like that that would prompt you.

379
00:41:06,528 --> 00:41:07,409
[SPEAKER_00]: You could already see

380
00:41:08,942 --> 00:41:15,845
[SPEAKER_00]: Slight inclinations, on such and such day, towards that or towards this, and you already know where it would lead.

381
00:41:16,325 --> 00:41:19,587
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you encounter the perceptions that will fuel it further or not, it's irrelevant.

382
00:41:21,268 --> 00:41:35,194
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why, that recognition, you measure what you need to apply, not what you think your duty is to apply, but by what current inclinations, mood, that the most general sense, the feeling, heart,

383
00:41:36,227 --> 00:41:37,067
[SPEAKER_00]: is leaning towards.

384
00:41:37,488 --> 00:41:39,709
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what your inclinations would be going towards.

385
00:41:40,689 --> 00:41:45,251
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by recognizing those inclinations, you're already recognizing the state of the animal.

386
00:41:46,572 --> 00:41:51,214
[SPEAKER_00]: What is kind of urging you for, or pushing you towards for, or trying to get you to do it for it.

387
00:41:52,174 --> 00:42:00,318
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why that that same recognition of your intentions kind of can be developed, can... well, certainly starts from the level of like a putujana,

388
00:42:01,227 --> 00:42:06,170
[SPEAKER_00]: Pretty much all the way up to arahantship would be the knowledge of your intentions and the reasons behind your actions.

389
00:42:06,250 --> 00:42:16,297
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, what thoughts have you been willingly accepting, fueling, inclining towards, that would then... that brought your mind to kind of lean in those directions even more.

390
00:42:17,377 --> 00:42:24,622
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as a householder or a monk, whenever you want to do this or that, you need to start looking at those peripheral reasons as to why I'm doing it.

391
00:42:24,642 --> 00:42:25,763
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I'm doing it because everybody says so.

392
00:42:25,803 --> 00:42:28,344
[SPEAKER_00]: No, but why am I doing it?

393
00:42:28,364 --> 00:42:28,865
[SPEAKER_00]: Even now.

394
00:42:29,600 --> 00:42:33,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I'm going to a monastery because everybody says that going to a monastery is a good thing.

395
00:42:33,863 --> 00:42:35,784
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, why am I doing it?

396
00:42:35,864 --> 00:42:53,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I'm doing it because, you know, it's a big festival and there's going to be hundreds of people and I'll get to mingle and meet everybody and feel justified in socializing and abandoning my seclusion and enjoying company and talking every day for a few days and it will all feel fine because it's, you know, in the monastery, it's for the dhamma, we're having a big festival and stuff.

397
00:42:53,795 --> 00:42:55,116
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, none of those reasons are wholesome.

398
00:42:56,172 --> 00:43:03,696
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're doing it for very central reasons, reasons of hindrances, reasons of restlessness, all externally, everybody would be praising you for it.

399
00:43:03,737 --> 00:43:04,037
[SPEAKER_00]: Wow!

400
00:43:04,197 --> 00:43:05,998
[SPEAKER_00]: You were so helpful, you were there all the time and stuff.

401
00:43:06,618 --> 00:43:19,526
[SPEAKER_00]: To the point that, I was saying that before to the monks, there's even that sutta where it says that Sotāpanna would not become an arahant, would block his own progress, basically, by sleeping too much, working too much, talking too much.

402
00:43:21,018 --> 00:43:23,639
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not kind of restraining himself in regard to those things.

403
00:43:24,040 --> 00:43:34,885
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the fourth reason would be getting engaged with dealing with like justifiable right saṅgha issues.

404
00:43:36,306 --> 00:43:39,948
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the Buddha said a monk who hasn't fulfilled his training should think

405
00:43:41,389 --> 00:43:59,777
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, like, when it comes to, obviously, the not-talk-too-much, not-sleep-too-much, not-work-too-much, as in, like, become completely distracted, but when it comes to the fourth one, you know, so, like, there might be justifiable threats to the saṅgha, to the buddhasāsana, to this and that, that monks have to address, have to deal with, have to engage with, he would still think, hold on,

406
00:44:00,607 --> 00:44:04,231
[SPEAKER_00]: There are monks in this assembly that finish their work, they're arahants.

407
00:44:04,731 --> 00:44:08,415
[SPEAKER_00]: Let them deal with it, because I still have the more important work to do.

408
00:44:08,835 --> 00:44:17,243
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even preventing, you know, saṅgha decline and whatnot, was not a justification for abandoning the mental training that leads to arahantry.

409
00:44:17,363 --> 00:44:19,165
[SPEAKER_00]: When you're an arahant, then you can go do what you want.

410
00:44:20,088 --> 00:44:23,909
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, obviously, there'll be certain things you will never be able to do again.

411
00:44:23,989 --> 00:44:25,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Like in the suttas, you know.

412
00:44:25,450 --> 00:44:30,651
[SPEAKER_00]: Either deal with the saṅgha or just go and roam around and talk to everybody and instruct the kings.

413
00:44:30,731 --> 00:44:33,332
[SPEAKER_00]: Or like that other arahant, they just built a whole village for people.

414
00:44:33,352 --> 00:44:39,414
[SPEAKER_00]: They built his kuti and they were living off of his support because he was getting so much support and...

415
00:44:39,614 --> 00:44:41,615
[SPEAKER_00]: He did the work.

416
00:44:41,695 --> 00:44:43,055
[SPEAKER_00]: None of that was a distraction for him.

417
00:44:43,295 --> 00:44:48,657
[SPEAKER_00]: But until that's the point that's developed, every intention matters.

418
00:44:49,763 --> 00:44:52,646
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might say, well, again, you know, this is a good thing to do.

419
00:44:53,046 --> 00:44:54,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I doing it?

420
00:44:54,847 --> 00:45:05,437
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you start applying restraint, letting go, observing with neutrality, on that level of intentions, you are getting on the level of ādicitta training.

421
00:45:06,558 --> 00:45:14,585
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, obviously, in order to get on that level, you would have had to be physically withdrawn, restrained, keeping the precepts for quite a bit of time.

422
00:45:15,879 --> 00:45:26,563
[SPEAKER_00]: So, any kind of wandering off in those coarsely unwholesome directions, there is a lot of space between.

423
00:45:26,603 --> 00:45:32,644
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you would see the animal way before it's full on in other people's pastures, so to speak.

424
00:45:32,825 --> 00:45:39,827
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why it's like the whole training of citta is measured by the state of citta and the state of citta is revealed through

425
00:45:40,787 --> 00:45:57,213
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, obviously, restraint, virtue, and then knowledge, and constant reflection on your intentions, actions by body, speech, and mind, and mentally, like, what thoughts do you choose to allow, welcome, delight in, so that they take hold of your mind even more.

426
00:45:57,553 --> 00:46:04,576
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't prevent them from arising, as the first sutta says, but you could certainly make sure you don't lose the right context that keeps them in the context.

427
00:46:05,933 --> 00:46:08,759
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... and that's pretty much the definition of the training.

428
00:46:08,999 --> 00:46:10,763
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, there's nothing else that you can do.

